messages:
kenny: Quick report from someone onboarding with the Java SDK sample: observed differences between the Kotlin sample and Java sample around timeouts + cancellation. The Java Retrofit setup seems to keep requests alive after worker cancel on Android 14.

leila: Oof. Can you paste the client configs for both samples?

kenny: Java sample (snippet):

```java
OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .retryOnConnectionFailure(true)
  .connectTimeout(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
  .readTimeout(0, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS) // streaming
  .dispatcher(new Dispatcher())
  .build();
```

Kotlin sample uses coroutine adapter + call.cancel() propagation. Java sample uses a Future wrapper that didn't cancel underlying Call properly.

omar: That explains why cancellation doesn't stop network; Future.cancel(false) won't interrupt the call thread. We should use call.cancel() when the Future is cancelled or switch to the same coroutine-based adapter.

nina: Also saw surprising retry behaviour: retryOnConnectionFailure=true plus high maxRequestsPerHost causes the queue to retry on flaky cellular and then all resume when signal returns; combined with non-singleflight token refresh this creates stampedes.

kenny: Onboarding note: the Java sample README doesn't mention that call.cancel() must be invoked on worker cancellation. Newcomer didn't expect to add that boilerplate. Feels like DX friction.

leila: Actionable items: (1) update Java sample to demonstrate proper cancellation (Future->call.cancel mapping), (2) add a small paragraph in README about retryOnConnectionFailure defaults and why the sample sets it false for upload-heavy flows, (3) add a parity test that verifies cancel semantics across sample variants.

omar: For Java cancellation snippet we recommend:

```java
Call call = service.upload(...);
CompletableFuture<Response> fut = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
  try { return call.execute(); } catch (IOException e) { throw new CompletionException(e); }
});
// when cancelling:
if (!fut.isDone()) {
  call.cancel();
  fut.cancel(true);
}
```

nina: Also suggest adding a small integration test that toggles connectivity (emulator shell) and asserts only one token refresh call when many uploads start. That will catch singleflight regressions.

kenny: One more Android 14 quirk: the default TLS 1.3 session resumption behavior changed and some OkHttp connection pooling metrics changed. We saw different connection reuse counts causing more connections and more auth headers being sent because the connection affinity changed. Add a note in changelog for Android 14 testing.

leila: Agreed. I'll open a docs PR adding a "Java sample parity" section, update the Java sample to use call.cancel integration, and add a coroutine-based alternative snippet. Also will lower maxRequestsPerHost in the sample client and set retryOnConnectionFailure(false).

ci-bot: ci: opened PR 3345 - sampleapp/java-cancel-parity. Tests queued on emulator-pixel6 and emulator-pixel8.

kenny: Thanks all. I'll run the PR through an end-to-end onboarding checklist (fresh clone, follow README steps) to verify newcomers don't hit this. If anyone else has quick devices to try Android 14 WiFi <-> mobile flaps, let me know. :pray:

leila: I'll test on Pixel 8 and post results. Also adding a tiny note in the SDK migration guide about call cancellation semantics.

omar: One-liner for commit messages: "samples: align Java sample cancellation with Kotlin adapter; lower dispatcher concurrency; doc call-cancel semantics"

kenny: Perfect. Closing the loop once CI passes.
